    "matchedParagraphs": [
      "For a changed method C#M, the selector chooses tests with an exact C#M edge. If no test in the entire map has that exact method edge, it escalates to tests with a class edge for C. A class-level change selects class-edge hits. Runnable tests represented with an empty footprint (NO_COVERAGE) remain conservative obligations. The extension collector (2e0954...) retains every successfully collected session with zero production coverage as such an entry; the original collector (70b398...) retained sessions whose report contained only non-production coverage but dropped sessions whose report contained no package. For the four original subjects, every PIT killing-identity occurrence in the KILLED records resolves to a map key, so no eligible record lost a reported killer through this difference. Invalid or unavailable state may trigger broader fallback. The mutation evaluator simulates one changed existing production method per record; it does not model additions, deletions, fields, initializers, or multi-method commits.",
      "The class-level baseline selects every mapped test with an edge to the mutated class and retains the same NO_COVERAGE obligations; it is a concrete same-map baseline, not a universal upper bound. The random equal-budget baseline samples the same number of logical tests as STP, using 1,000 Monte Carlo trials (seed 42); the analytical expectation is within 0.1 percentage points of the Monte Carlo value for every subject.",
      "The original Commons Lang, JGraphT, spring-core, and PetClinic artifacts retain weaker historical sample provenance. Flink, Spring Security, Hibernate, and Quarkus prospectively froze subject metadata, samples, and functional targetTests policies before result inspection. Project-specific PIT scopes bound the operational oracle and can omit cross-scope killers. The original and extension groups also use different recorded collector revisions (70b398... and 2e0954...). Between them, three commits touch collection or conversion: deterministic map ordering, retention of zero-coverage sessions (Section 3.2), and per-session execution-identity metadata. None changes JaCoCo probe edges or the name-level method key. Project characteristics and collection revision therefore co-vary. RQ4 describes recurrence under these configurations; it is not a controlled estimate of project effects or evidence that a single unchanged collector generalizes across both groups.",
      "The latter study exposed a separate implementation filter defect: a package segment named .test. caused legitimate main-output classes to be discarded even though raw JaCoCo XML contained them. A later source-output ownership fix changed 110 affected identities from false NO_COVERAGE to mapped coverage. Semantic comparison preserved the frozen 419-record stream-study inputs, so PIT was not rerun. This does not establish that the eight historical maps would be unchanged by the fix. However, no canonical map of the eight subjects contains a production class whose package has a test segment, and no such class exists in the qualified production source scope of the seven subjects whose recorded source revision was available (spring-core's revision was not retained). Neither follow-up reproduces or explains Dreier\u2019s historical mutants."
    ],
